07c.943.944 2.199 1.465 3.535 1.465s2.592-.521 3.535-1.465L19.071 12a5.008 5.008 0 0 0 0-7.071 5.006 5.006 0 0 0-7.071 0z"></path></svg><a href="https://daytripper28.com" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" class="text-blue-500 text-xs">daytripper28.com</a></p></div></div><div class="text-black/60 font-medium mb-1">Minnesota</div><h1 class="text-3xl font-extrabold mb-3 text-black/80 break-words">Minnesota&#x27;s Controversial Confederate Relic: The 28th Virginia Battle Flag</h1><div class="flex flex-row items-center text-black/60"><span class="ml-1">2023-09-22</span></div><section><div style="max-height:none;overflow:auto"><div><div class="jsx-87703400 NewsContent_content__8jcWa content"><div class="jsx-777308592 jsx-3381000567 content"><p class="NBAIEditor_Theme__paragraph" dir="ltr"><span>Tucked away in a drawer at the </span><a href="https://mnhs.gitlab.io/archive/education-resources/www.mnhs.org/education/resources/28th-virginia-battle-flag.html" rel="noopener" class="NBAIEditor_Theme__link"><span>Minnesota Historical Society</span></a><span> lies an obscure yet controversial artifact - a Confederate battle flag captured by a Minnesota regiment during the decisive Battle of Gettysburg.</span></p><p class="NBAIEditor_Theme__paragraph" dir="ltr"><span>This relic, the 28th Virginia Infantry flag, has been at the center of an ongoing dispute about whether it rightfully belongs to Minnesota or should be returned to its native Virginia.</span></p><figure><div data-editornodeinfo="%7B%22type%22%3A%22editor-image-node%22%2C%22imageUrl%22%3A%22https%3A%2F%2Fi.prt.news%2Fac7e3a4978c703e5184d76ad392f5229.blob%22%2C%22imageCaption%22%3A%2228th%20Virginia%20Infantry%20Color%20captured%20at%20Gettysburg%20by%20Private%20Marshall%20Sherman%2C%20Company%20C%2C%201st%20Minnesota%20Infantry%22%2C%22creditText%22%3A%22Public%20Domain%22%2C%22creditUrl%22%3A%22https%3A%2F%2Fcommons.wikimedia.org%2Fwiki%2FFile%3A28th_Virginia_Infantry_Color.jpg%3Fuselang%3Den%23Licensing%22%2C%22imagePlatform%22%3A%22SELF_UPLOAD%22%2C%22imageOriginalWidth%22%3A591%2C%22imageOriginalHeight%22%3A558%7D"><div class="editor-image-wrap"><div class="editor-image"><img class="" src="https://img.particlenews.com/image.php?type=thumbnail_580x000&amp;url=1dkMNN_0oeykjac00" data-image-caption="28th Virginia Infantry Color captured at Gettysburg by Private Marshall Sherman, Company C, 1st Minnesota Infantry" data-credit-text="Public Domain" data-credit-url="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:28th_Virginia_Infantry_Color.jpg?uselang=en#Licensing" data-image-platform="SELF_UPLOAD" data-image-original-width="591" data-image-original-height="558" alt="https://img.particlenews.com/image.php?url=1dkMNN_0oeykjac00" width="591" height="558"/></div><span class="image-introduction-wrap"><span class="image-caption">28th Virginia Infantry Color captured at Gettysburg by Private Marshall Sherman, Company C, 1st Minnesota Infantry</span><span class="photo-by">Photo by</span><span class="credit-text"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:28th_Virginia_Infantry_Color.jpg?uselang=en#Licensing" target="_blank">Public Domain</a></span></span></div></div></figure><h2 class="NBAIEditor_Theme__h2" dir="ltr"><span>Spoils of War</span></h2><p class="NBAIEditor_Theme__paragraph" dir="ltr" style="text-align:start"><span>The flag was seized in 1863 by the courageous 1st Minnesota Infantry, who suffered immense casualties staving off Confederate forces at Gettysburg. </span></p><p class="NBAIEditor_Theme__paragraph" dir="ltr"><span>Private Marshall Sherman retrieved the dropped banner, earning himself a Medal of Honor.</span></p><p class="NBAIEditor_Theme__paragraph" dir="ltr" style="text-align:start"><span>After the war, the flag passed from federal to state hands, exhibited periodically at Minnesota&#x27;s capitol. </span></p><p class="NBAIEditor_Theme__paragraph" dir="ltr"><span>It became part of the Historical Society&#x27;s permanent collection in 1896.</span></p><h2 class="NBAIEditor_Theme__h2" dir="ltr"><span>The Controversy</span></h2><p class="NBAIEditor_Theme__paragraph" dir="ltr" style="text-align:start"><span>But since 1905, Virginia has argued that Confederate flags should be repatriated to their states of origin.</span></p><p class="NBAIEditor_Theme__paragraph" dir="ltr"><span>Requests for the 28th Virginia flag&#x27;s return have been made in 1961, 1998, 2000, and 2013, only to be refused each time by Minnesota governors and state officials.</span></p><h2 class="NBAIEditor_Theme__h2" dir="ltr"><span>Symbolizes Sacrifice</span></h2><p class="NBAIEditor_Theme__paragraph" dir="ltr" style="text-align:start"><span>To Minnesotans, the flag symbolizes a brave regiment&#x27;s heroic sacrifice. Some oppose returning it on principle, or to avoid glorifying the Confederate cause.</span></p><p class="NBAIEditor_Theme__paragraph" dir="ltr"><span>Officials also argue Minnesota has legal ownership after more than 150 years.</span></p><p class="NBAIEditor_Theme__paragraph" dir="ltr" style="text-align:start"><span>Meanwhile, many in Virginia view reclaiming the banner as a point of state pride, honoring those who fought and died under its red silk and blue cross.</span></p><p class="NBAIEditor_Theme__paragraph" dir="ltr"><span>While controversial as a Confederate emblem, to descendants it represents family heritage.</span></p><figure><div data-editornodeinfo="%7B%22type%22%3A%22editor-image-node%22%2C%22imageUrl%22%3A%22https%3A%2F%2Fi.prt.news%2F8bb894019cc3037da0e8b25cb23d857e.blob%22%2C%22imageCaption%22%3A%22Marshall%20Sherman%20poses%20with%20the%20flag%20in%201864%22%2C%22creditText%22%3A%22Public%20Domain%22%2C%22creditUrl%22%3A%22https%3A%2F%2Fcommons.wikimedia.org%2Fwiki%2FFile%3AMarshall_Sherman_with_28th_Virginia_battle_flag.jpg%3Fuselang%3Den%23Licensing%22%2C%22imagePlatform%22%3A%22SELF_UPLOAD%22%2C%22imageOriginalWidth%22%3A401%2C%22imageOriginalHeight%22%3A640%7D"><div class="editor-image-wrap"><div class="editor-image"><img class="" src="https://img.particlenews.com/image.php?type=thumbnail_580x000&amp;url=1lBcyt_0oeykjac00" data-image-caption="Marshall Sherman poses with the flag in 1864" data-credit-text="Public Domain" data-credit-url="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marshall_Sherman_with_28th_Virginia_battle_flag.jpg?uselang=en#Licensing" data-image-platform="SELF_UPLOAD" data-image-original-width="401" data-image-original-height="640" alt="https://img.particlenews.com/image.php?url=1lBcyt_0oeykjac00" width="401" height="640"/></div><span class="image-introduction-wrap"><span class="image-caption">Marshall Sherman poses with the flag in 1864</span><span class="photo-by">Photo by</span><span class="credit-text"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marshall_Sherman_with_28th_Virginia_battle_flag.jpg?uselang=en#Licensing" target="_blank">Public Domain</a></span></span></div></div></figure><h2 class="NBAIEditor_Theme__h2" dir="ltr"><span>A Relic</span></h2><p class="NBAIEditor_Theme__paragraph" dir="ltr" style="text-align:start"><span>The 28th Virginia flag remains one of the last unresolved captives of Gettysburg.</span></p><p class="NBAIEditor_Theme__paragraph" dir="ltr"><span>While other states have returned their Confederate relics, Minnesota still guards its hard-earned memento despite protests from Virginia.</span></p><p class="NBAIEditor_Theme__paragraph" dir="ltr"><span>The two states remain unlikely to find compromise on this living legacy of America&#x27;s fractured past.</span></p><p class="NBAIEditor_Theme__paragraph"><br/></p><style style="display:none">.NBAIEditor_Theme__ol1 {padding: 0 0 0 20px !important;margin: 0 0 24px 0 !important;list-style-position: outside !important;}.NBAIEditor_Theme__ol2 {padding: 0 !important;margin: 0 !important;list-style-type: lower-alpha !important;list-style-position: outside !important;}html body.post #content-div .content ol li ol {margin: 0 !important;}.NBAIEditor_Theme__ol3 {padding: 0 !important;margin: 0 !important;list-