.com","scope":0,"scope_web":0,"share_count":0,"show_as_bloom_ads":false,"source":"DayTripper","summary":"Saturday morning, June 20, thousands of runners will line up in Two Harbors and begin one of Minnesota's most beloved traditions: Grandma's Marathon. The 50th annual race marks a milestone for what started as a tiny local event with just 160 runners in 1977.","tagline":"Writer","text_category":{},"title":"Grandma's Marathon Turns 50: Why This Weekend Is Huge for Duluth","up":1,"url":"https://h5.newsbreakapp.com/mp/1CT3vFx9?cv=\u0026platform=2","url_before_mp_full_article":"https://mp.newsbreakapp.com/post/165062558?sig=08F25B6FBF287AF1972F4CEF80B7D5E2","video_category":{},"video_metadata":{"default_cover_with_play_icon_cdn_url":"","default_first_frame_cdn_url":""},"viewType":"web","vote":0},{"ai_summary":"","amp":"https://mp.newsbreakapp.com/post/165062113?sig=0F4A157E0120482898B57C7DDC453243","audio_file":"","audio_length_sec":0,"canonical_url":"https://mp.newsbreakapp.com/post/165062113?sig=0F4A157E0120482898B57C7DDC453243","certifications_badges":[{"dark_color":"#FF5A5A","dark_icon":"https://i.prt.news/t_200_200_ff128e59e44fd908bc949f34bde102c1.png","description":"Users in NewsBreak’s Contributor Program who have verified their accounts by showing some writing experience on other platforms.","es_description":"Usuarios en el Programa de Colaboradores de NewsBreak que han verificado sus cuentas mostrando algo de experiencia en redacción en otras plataformas.","light_color":"#FF5A5A","light_icon":"https://i.prt.news/t_200_200_ff128e59e44fd908bc949f34bde102c1.png","name":"writer","text":"Writer","type":"certification"}],"city_name":"Minnesota","comment_count":0,"comment_user_count":0,"content":"\u003cbody\u003e\u003cp class=\"NBAIEditor_Theme__paragraph\" dir=\"ltr\"\u003e\u003cspan\u003eOn a humid summer day in 1931, a highway construction crew near Pelican Rapids made a discovery that would rewrite Minnesota's ancient history. As workers graded a frost-heave bump in the road, a road grader suddenly hit something hard—a human skull, empty-eyed and impossibly old.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp class=\"NBAIEditor_Theme__paragraph\" dir=\"ltr\"\u003e\u003cspan\u003eWhat the crew didn't know was that they'd unearthed one of North America's oldest documented human remains. For 45 years, she was known as \"Minnesota Man,\" but in 1976, archaeologists finally realized the truth: the skeleton belonged to a young woman who lived 8,000 to 10,000 years ago (possibly even 20,000 years old).\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp class=\"NBAIEditor_Theme__paragraph\" dir=\"ltr\"\u003e\u003cspan\u003eToday, June 18 marks the anniversary of that remarkable find, and her story still captivates archaeologists worldwide.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cfigure\u003e\u003cdiv data-editornodeinfo=\"%7B%22type%22%3A%22editor-image-node%22%2C%22imageUrl%22%3A%22https%3A%2F%2Fi.prt.news%2F16bf8adb9e765124faaf85bfcb7f3c98.blob%22%2C%22imageCaption%22%3A%22%22%2C%22creditText%22%3A%22Pleistocene%20Man%20in%20Minnesota%2C%20Univ.%20of%20Minn.%20Press.%201936%22%2C%22creditUrl%22%3A%22%22%2C%22imagePlatform%22%3A%22SELF_UPLOAD%22%2C%22imageOriginalWidth%22%3A400%2C%22imageOriginalHeight%22%3A502%7D\"\u003e\u003cdiv class=\"editor-image-wrap\"\u003e\u003cdiv class=\"editor-image\"\u003e\u003cimg class=\"\" src=\"https://img.particlenews.com/image.php?type=thumbnail_580x000\u0026amp;url=36V0xY_1CT2qVit00\" data-image-caption=\"\" data-credit-text=\"Pleistocene Man in Minnesota, Univ. of Minn. Press. 1936\" data-credit-url=\"\" data-image-platform=\"SELF_UPLOAD\" data-image-original-width=\"400\" data-image-original-height=\"502\" alt=\"https://img.particlenews.com/image.php?url=36V0xY_1CT2qVit00\" width=\"400\" height=\"502\" \u003e\u003c/div\u003e\u003cspan class=\"image-introduction-wrap\"\u003e\u003cspan class=\"photo-by\"\u003ePhoto by\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"credit-text\"\u003ePleistocene Man in Minnesota, Univ. of Minn. Press. 1936\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\u003c/figure\u003e\u003ch2 class=\"NBAIEditor_Theme__h2\" dir=\"ltr\"\u003e\u003cspan\u003eAn Ancient Mystery\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/h2\u003e\u003cp class=\"NBAIEditor_Theme__paragraph\" dir=\"ltr\"\u003e\u003cspan\u003eThe skeleton told a surprising story about life in prehistoric Minnesota. The pelvis indicated she was a mature woman, yet she'd never borne children. Most remarkably, she wasn't buried alone—two artifacts lay beside her bones: a conch shell pendant and a dagger carved from an elk's horn.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp class=\"NBAIEditor_Theme__paragraph\" dir=\"ltr\"\u003e\u003cspan\u003eThe conch shell is particularly striking. Its species exists naturally only in Florida, suggesting this ancient Minnesotan was connected to a vast trade network spanning thousands of miles across the continent. In an age before roads or written language, shells from the Gulf somehow made their way to the lakes region, a testament to the sophistication of these early peoples.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp class=\"NBAIEditor_Theme__paragraph\" dir=\"ltr\"\u003e\u003cspan\u003eUniversity of Minnesota archaeologist Dr. Albert Jenks recognized the find's immense importance immediately. This wasn't just a skeleton—it was proof that humans had thrived in Minnesota for far longer than most scientists of the 1930s believed possible.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003ch2 class=\"NBAIEditor_Theme__h2\" dir=\"ltr\"\u003e\u003cspan\u003eFrom \"Man\" to Woman\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/h2\u003e\u003cp class=\"NBAIEditor_Theme__paragraph\" dir=\"ltr\"\u003e\u003cspan\u003eThe discovery challenged everything academics thought they knew about human migration and settlement in North America. Yet for decades, the historical plaque outside Pelican Rapids called her \"Minnesota Man.\" It wasn't until 1976 that historians and anthropologists officially corrected the name to \"Minnesota Woman,\" finally giving this ancient Minnesotan her rightful identity.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp class=\"NBAIEditor_Theme__paragraph\" dir=\"ltr\"\u003e\u003cspan\u003eShe remains one of the most significant archaeological discoveries in the state. Her bones, her artifacts, and the questions they raise continue to intrigue researchers who study how humans first came to Minnesota and how they survived in this harsh landscape.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003ch2 class=\"NBAIEditor_Theme__h2\" dir=\"ltr\"\u003e\u003cspan\u003eVisit the History\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/h2\u003e\u003cp class=\"NBAIEditor_Theme__paragraph\" dir=\"ltr\"\u003e\u003cspan\u003eYou can learn more about Minnesota Woman and other prehistoric discoveries at the Minnesota History Center in St. Paul. The Minnesota Historical Society maintains detailed records of this find and its significance to understanding our shared past.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp class=\"NBAIEditor_Theme__paragraph\" dir=\"ltr\"\u003e\u003cspan\u003eFor more information, visit the \u003c/span\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://ottertailcounty.gov/history/the-minnesota-woman/\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"NBAIEditor_Theme__link\"\u003e\u003cspan\u003eOtter Tail County history page\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\u003cspan\u003e or the \u003c/span\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://www.mnhs.org/\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"NBAIEditor_Theme__link\"\u003e\u003cspan\u003eMinnesota Historical Society\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\u003cspan\u003e. Pelican Rapids itself has a fascinating history you can explore during a visit to this small Minnesota town.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp class=\"NBAIEditor_Theme__paragraph\"\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cstyle style=\"display: none\"\u003e.NBAIEditor_Theme__ol1 {padding: 0 0 0 20px !important;margin: 0 0 24px 0 !important;list-style-position: outside !important;}.NBAIEditor_Theme__ol2 {padding: 0 !important;margin: 0 !important;list-style-type: lower-alpha !important;list-style-pos